vantage. I have a lot of defensive power in my hand, but not much offensive firepower beyond the bomb.\n\nThe real strategic play here is to *trust* my teammate. They took the initiative with the Kings, and the Landlord didn't challenge. My teammate likely has a plan. Forcing me to play would disrupt their flow. I need to be ready to provide defensive support or to make the game flow more fluidly with a good offensive play.\n\nSo, the optimal move is to *pass*. I allow my teammate to continue, hopefully executing their strategy effectively. It’s the highest probability play based on the current board. I will keep my high cards for later to defend against the Landlord.\n\n\n", 'output': 'Evaluation: The current play on the table is a pair of Kings by my teammate, and the landlord has passed. My teammate is showing strength and taking the initiative. My hand contains loose singles (6, 7, 8, 10) which are hard to clear, along with strong defensive cards (JJJJ, AA, Red Joker). Taking the lead with AA would force me to lead a weak hand, likely resulting in me losing control or wasting high cards. Since the landlord has already passed, letting my teammate win this trick allows them to dictate the next move, which is the optimal cooperative strategy.\n\nReason: My teammate played a high pair (KK) to beat my previous low pair, indicating they want control. The landlord passed, signaling weakness or a desire to hold cards. Beating my teammate\'s KK with my AA would be counterproductive as it wastes my high cards against an ally and forces me to lead from a hand with unconnected low singles. I should save my AA, Red Joker, and Bomb to defend against the Landlord later.\n\nAnswer: {"cards": []}'}
